<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work: everyone does this</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">Predict first</div>
    <p>Tōhoku 2011 was a magnitude 9.1 quake offshore of Japan. San Andreas quakes happen on land, along a strike-slip fault. Predict: which one produces a tsunami, and what does the answer depend on? Write your prediction in your Field Notebook before you read or watch.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout task">
    <div class="callout-title">The task: Same planet, different hazards</div>
    <p>In four steps each, explain (a) why a subduction earthquake can make a tsunami, and (b) why an earthquake on a fault that slides sideways usually does not. Use one real event as your example for each. The <a href="/reference/case-studies/">Case Study Library</a> has nine.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout support">
    <div class="callout-title">If you want support</div>
    <p>The <a href="/reference/case-studies/tohoku-2011/">Tōhoku 2011 case study page</a> and the <a href="/foundations/eal/u3/">vocabulary page</a>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout extend">
    <div class="callout-title">If you want to go further</div>
    <p>Compare <a href="/reference/case-studies/tohoku-2011/">Tōhoku 2011</a> and <a href="/reference/case-studies/kobe-1995/">Kobe 1995</a>. Same country, 16 years apart, very different hazards. Why?</p>
